        },
        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pinpoint (petechia) or larger (ecchymosis) hemorrhage in the skin or mucous membranes most often secondary to abnormal primary hemostasis (platelet or vessel wall-mediated); may appear spontaneously or following trauma. Spontan eous development often occurs at sites of increased capillary trauma.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thrombocytopenia results in impaired primary hemostasis and decreased production of platelet-derived trophic factors that maintain endothelial cell junction integrity.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Platelet numbers below 50 × 103/μL and more often below 25 × 103/μL are associated with an increased risk of spontaneous hemorrhage.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thrombocytopathies are not generally associated with the development of petechiae. Some animals with von Willebrand disease (VWD) may develop petechiae.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vascular hemostatic defects—generally caused by systemic disease causing increased capillary permeability, vasculitis, or altered dermal vascular support (e.g., collagen alterations).",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gastrointestinal—melena/hematochezia/hematemesis.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemic/lymphatic/immune—anemia from bleeding.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neurologic—variable depending on location of bleeding.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ophthalmic—scleral/retinal hemorrhage, hyphema, secondary glaucoma, and uveitis.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal/urologic—hematuria.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Respiratory—epistaxis/hemoptysis.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Skin/exocrine—petechia/ecchymosis/bruising.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GENETICS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "GENETICS",
          "canonical_heading": "GENETICS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most etiologies are acquired; immunemediated thrombocytopenia (ITP) has a possible genetic predisposition in cocker spaniels, poodles, Maltese, and Old English sheepdogs. Genetic associations exist for many thrombocytopathies. VWD is seen in Doberman pinschers, German shorthaired pointers, Shetland sheepdogs, Scottish terriers, and Chesapeake Bay retrievers.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "INCIDENCE/PREVELENCE",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Common in dogs. Uncommon in cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1154,
              "printed_page": 1072,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "INCIDENCE/PREVELENCE"
            }
          ]
        },
